<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Actium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">31 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/final-war-of-the-roman-republic">Final War of the Roman Republic</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Actium was a naval battle fought in 31 BCE off western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> between Octavian and the combined forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a>. Octavian's fleet under Agrippa won decisively. The victory led to the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>, and it made Octavian, the future Augustus, sole ruler of the Roman world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium.jpg" alt="Battle of Actium" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark Landon · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medinaceli_Actium_reliefs_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
